### 14.2 Actual Crack Path Mesh Resolution Audit ($h / l_0$)
To determine why the adaptive solution exhibits strong post-peak load drop ($-44.31\%$) and kink angle deviation ($+7.50^\circ$), the mesh resolution was quantified directly along the active crack path arc length $s$:

| Mesh Discretization | Points on Path | Arc Length ($s_{\max}$) | Min $h$ (mm) | Mean $h$ (mm) | Max $h$ (mm) | Min $h/l_0$ | Mean $h/l_0$ | Median $h/l_0$ | Max $h/l_0$ | Fraction $h/l_0 < 0.25$ | Fraction $0.25 \le h/l_0 < 0.50$ | Fraction $h/l_0 \ge 0.50$ |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **$H_1$ Reference** | 23 | $0.4648	ext{ mm}$ | $0.00306$ | $0.00705$ | $0.00791$ | $0.204$ | $0.470$ | $0.523$ | $0.527$ | $8.7\%$ | $21.7\%$ | $69.6\%$ |
| **$H_2$ Ultrafine** | 23 | $0.4648	ext{ mm}$ | $0.00184$ | $0.00438$ | $0.00500$ | $0.123$ | $0.292$ | $0.332$ | $0.333$ | $21.7\%$ | $78.3\%$ | **$0.0\%$** |
| **Adaptive Candidate** | 23 | $0.4648	ext{ mm}$ | **$0.00238$** | $0.00700$ | **$0.01073$** | **$0.159$** | $0.467$ | $0.533$ | **$0.716$** | $8.7\%$ | $39.1\%$ | **$52.2\%$** |

**Correlation Finding:**
- Near the notch tip ($s \le 0.05	ext{ mm}$), the adaptive mesh achieves fine resolution ($h_{\min} = 0.00238	ext{ mm}, h/l_0 = 0.159$).
- Downstream along the curved crack path ($s > 0.15	ext{ mm}, x > 0.20	ext{ mm}$), the adaptive element size grows to $h = 0.01073	ext{ mm}$ ($h/l_0 = 0.716$). Over **$52.2\%$** of the crack path length lies in the under-resolved zone ($h/l_0 \ge 0.50$).
- This downstream de-refinement coincides spatially with the regions of highest post-peak compliance and softer residual load. We report this strictly as **correlation evidence**, preserving that causality requires single-factor mesh refinement verification.

### 14.3 Publication-Facing Comparison with Pandey & Kumar (2025)
A reproducible digitization record was established for Pandey & Kumar (2025) Fig. 13(a) (`pandey_kumar_2025_fig13a_digitized.csv`), classified strictly as `DIGITIZED_APPROXIMATE`:

| Characteristic | Published Standard PFM (~37k) | Published Proposed PFM (~20k) | Project $H_1$ Reference (12,064) | Project Adaptive (7,865) | Literature Alignment Note |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Pre-Peak Slope** | $\sim 12.8	ext{ kN/mm}$ | $\sim 12.8	ext{ kN/mm}$ | $12.83	ext{ kN/mm}$ | $12.80	ext{ kN/mm}$ | Identical elastic response |
| **Peak Load ($F_{\max}$)** | $\sim 0.1435	ext{ kN}$ | $\sim 0.1455	ext{ kN}$ | $0.1437	ext{ kN}$ | $0.1462	ext{ kN}$ | Near-perfect peak match ($<0.5\%$ error) |
| **Displacement at Peak** | $\sim 0.0125	ext{ mm}$ | $\sim 0.0128	ext{ mm}$ | $0.0125	ext{ mm}$ | $0.0131	ext{ mm}$ | Consistent kinematics |
| **Force at $u=0.015	ext{ mm}$** | $\sim 0.1180	ext{ kN}$ | $\sim 0.1120	ext{ kN}$ | $0.1197	ext{ kN}$ | $0.1165	ext{ kN}$ | Adaptive relaxes below reference |
| **Force at $u=0.020	ext{ mm}$** | $\sim 0.0480	ext{ kN}$ | $\sim 0.0380	ext{ kN}$ | $0.0485	ext{ kN}$ | $0.0398	ext{ kN}$ | Close match to published proposed curve |
| **Force at $u=0.030	ext{ mm}$** | $\sim 0.0180	ext{ kN}$ | $\sim 0.0140	ext{ kN}$ | $0.0247	ext{ kN}$ | $0.0138	ext{ kN}$ | Captures published lower post-peak trend |
| **Published Domain Range**| $u \le 0.035	ext{ mm}$ | $u \le 0.035	ext{ mm}$ | $u \le 0.050	ext{ mm}$ | $u \le 0.050	ext{ mm}$ | Project solved beyond published limit |

**Authoritative Literature Context:**
- Pandey & Kumar explicitly state that *"the proposed solution relaxes somewhat more than the standard solution beyond approximately $0.015	ext{ mm}$"*.
- The project adaptive candidate closely mirrors this relaxation behavior up to $u pprox 0.030	ext{ mm}$. However, beyond $0.030	ext{ mm}$, where the published curve terminates, the project adaptive curve drops to $0.0078	ext{ kN}$ at $u=0.04258	ext{ mm}$ ($-44.31\%$ vs $H_1$).
- While qualitatively aligned with the author's observed trend, this qualitative statement cannot be used to justify an arbitrarily large numerical difference. Therefore, the post-peak response is classified as `MESH-SENSITIVE`.

### 14.4 Computational Cost & Solver Burden Audit (Separating Discretization from Cost)
A lower element count establishes strictly `LOWER_ELEMENT_COUNT`, not computational efficiency:

| Execution Metric | $H_1$ Reference (Job `1408543`) | $H_2$ Ultrafine (Job `1408550`) | Adaptive Candidate (Job `1408555`) | Relative Delta (Adaptive vs $H_1$) | Epistemological Classification |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Underlying Finite Elements** | 12,064 | 33,852 | **7,865** | **$-34.8\%$ elements** | `LOWER_ELEMENT_COUNT` |
| **Total Abaqus Element Objects** | 36,192 | 101,556 | **23,595** | **$-34.8\%$ solver objects** | `LOWER_ELEMENT_COUNT` |
| **Accepted Increments** | 144 | 105 (cutback limit) | **358** | **$+148.6\%$ increments** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **Total Attempts** | 151 | 118 | **396** | **$+162.3\%$ attempts** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **Solver Cutbacks** | 14 | 20 | **73** | **$+421.4\%$ cutbacks** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **Equilibrium Iterations** | 646 | 462 | **1,713** | **$+165.2\%$ iterations** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **CPU Time (seconds)** | 586 s (09m 46s) | 1,192 s (19m 52s) | **1,002 s (16m 42s)** | **$+70.99\%$ CPU time** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **Walltime (seconds)** | 636 s (10m 36s) | 1,274 s (21m 14s) | **1,197 s (19m 57s)** | **$+88.21\%$ walltime** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |
| **Peak Memory Allocation** | 667,604 KB (~652 MB) | 1,482,012 KB (~1.41 GB) | **1,752,668 KB (~1.67 GB)** | **$+162.5\%$ peak memory** | `HIGHER_SOLVER_BURDEN` |

**Conclusion:** The adaptive remeshed model required almost double the walltime ($19	ext{m }57	ext{s}$ vs $10	ext{m }36	ext{s}$) and over $2.6	imes$ more equilibrium iterations and peak memory than $H_1$, due to severe localized cutbacks as the damage zone traversed non-uniform element boundaries. Any claim of "computational efficiency gained" is refuted by empirical solver telemetry.

